From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 09:20:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, gdi32, wow64win: D3DKMT synchronization objects:
 Create, Open, Destroy, Signal, Wait (3323)

CORE review: graphics. D3DKMTCreateSynchronizationObject(2),
OpenSynchronizationObject, OpenSyncObjectFromNtHandle(2),
OpenSyncObjectNtHandleFromName and DestroySynchronizationObject were stubs,
and D3DKMTSignalSynchronizationObjectFromCpu and
D3DKMTWaitForSynchronizationObjectFromCpu did not exist.

A synchronization object (mutex, semaphore, fence, CPU notification, monitored
fence) is a block in a section like the resources of 3321: its type, limit and
value are in the header, so a shared one is the same object in every process
(a global share handle, an NT handle, a named NT handle, or an NT handle
together with a resource: D3DKMTShareObjects, OpenResourceFromNtHandle gives
it back as hSyncObject). A monitored fence's CPU address is the value itself,
mapped below 2 GB in a 32-bit process. Signal sets a fence to a value (never
down unless AllowFenceRewind), adds one to a semaphore (up to its maximum),
signals a mutex or sets the event of a CPU notification; Wait waits for all of
the objects, or any of them, taking a mutex or semaphore it ends on.

New syscalls: win32u.spec, gdi32.spec, ntgdi.h, main.c, the wow64 thunks and
win32syscalls.h (regenerated with tools/make_specfiles). The D3DKMT structures
with 64-bit members are 8-byte aligned in 32-bit builds now, as Windows' own
compiler lays them out, which is what the wow64 thunks read.

